  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\sqrt{\\frac{6}{7p}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{6}{7p}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{7p}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{7p}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7p},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{7p}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7p}}{\\sqrt{7p}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{42p}}{(\\sqrt{7p})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{42p}}{7p}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
